¶
{{smaller block|I cannot give you over thus ; I most earnestly implore you that you would not deferre to consider yourselfe ''throughly.''{{right|''N. Ward'', Simple Cobler, p. 65.}}}}

{{smaller block|Truly and ''throughly'' to live up to the principles of their religion.{{right|''Tillotson.''}}}}

'''through-mortise''' (thrö′môr″tis), ''n.'' A mortise which passes entirely through the timber in which it is made.

'''throughout''' (thrö-out′), ''adv.'' and ''prep.'' [< ''through''<sup>1</sup> + ''out.'' Cf. ''thoroughout.''] '''I.''' ''adv.'' Everywhere; in every part; in all respects.

{{smaller block|His youth and age,
All of a piece ''throughout'', and all divine. ''Dryden.''}}

{{smaller block|His conduct ''throughout'' was equally defective in principle and in sound policy.{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., ii. 1.}}}}

'''II.''' ''prep.'' Quite through; from one end or side of to the other; in every part of.

{{smaller block|There is not that thing in the world of more grave and urgent importance ''throughout'' the whole life of man, then is discipline.{{right|''Milton'', Church-Government, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|''Meer.'' The thing is for recovery of drowned land. . . .
''Eng. Thoroughout'' England.{{right|''B. Jonson'', Devil is an Ass, ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|The conflict lasted ''throughout'' the night, with carnage on both sides.{{right|''Irving'', Granada, p. 60.}}}}

'''throughoutly†''' (thrö-out′li), ''adv.'' [< ME. *''throughoutly, throughtly'' ; < ''throughout'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Throughout; completely.

{{smaller block|And so huge a stroke geuyng hym was tho,
That quite clene the arme share off ''throughtly.''{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3075.}}}}

{{smaller block|If this first worke bee throughly and ''throughoutly'' dispatched, as I hope it is, the great Remora is removed.{{right|''N. Ward'', Simple Cobler, p. 36.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
= Sw. ''dreja'' = Dan. ''dreje'' = Goth. *''thraian'' (not recorded), turn. Hence ult. ''thread''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To turn; twist; specifically, to form into threads by twisting two or more filaments together, or by twisting two or more singles together in a direction contrary to the twist of the singles themselves: as, to ''throw'' silk: sometimes applied in a wide sense to the whole series of operations by which silk is prepared for the weaver.

{{smaller block|The art of spinning and ''throwing'' silk had been introduced [into England in 1455] by a company of silk women, of what country is not known. {{right|''A. Barlow'', Weaving, p. 18.}}}}

'''2.''' To shape on a potters' wheel. {{smaller|The mass of clay revolves under the hands of the potter, who gives it the desired form. See ''thrown ware'', under ''thrown''.}} '''3'''†. To fashion by turning on a lathe; turn.— '''4.''' To cast; heave; pitch; toss; fling: literally or figuratively: as, to ''throw'' a stone at a bird.

{{smaller block|Sothely the boot in the mydil see was ''throwen'' with waiwis, forsothe the wynd was contrarie.
{{right|''Wyclif'', Mat. xiv. 24.}}
''Throw'' physic to the dogs; I'll none of it.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, v. 3. 47.}}
This day was the sayd Anthonie Gelber sowed in a Chauina filled with stones, and ''throwen'' into the sea.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', II. 110.}}
Scurrility! That is he that ''throweth'' scandals—
Soweth and ''throweth'' scandals, as 'twere dirt,
Even in the face of holiness and devotion.
{{right|''Randolph'', Muses' Looking Glass, iv. 5.}}
The contempt he ''throws'' upon them in another passage is yet more remarkable. {{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No. 135.}}}}

'''5.''' To cast with sudden force or violence; impel violently; hurl; dash: as, the shock ''threw'' the wall down.

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|What tempest, I trow, ''threw'' this whale . . . ashore at Windsor?
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., ii. 1. 65.}}}}

{{smaller block|Each sudden passion ''throws'' me where it lists,<br>And overwhelms all that oppose my will.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', King and No King, iv. 4.}}}}

6. To fling; floor; give a fall to, as in wrestling; unhorse, as in justing.

{{smaller block|Charles in a moment ''threw'' him, and broke three of his ribs.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, i. 2. 135.}}}}

7. To unseat and bring to the ground.

{{smaller block|If a nag is to ''throw'' me, I say, let him have some blood.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Felix Holt, xx.}}}}

8. To cast; shed.

{{smaller block|There the snake ''throws'' her enamell'd skin.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., ii. 1. 255.}}}}

9. To spread or put on carelessly or hurriedly: as, to ''throw'' a shawl over one's shoulders.

{{smaller block|I have seen her . . . ''throw'' her nightgown upon her.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, v. 1. 5.}}}}

10. To advance or place quickly, as by some rapid movement.

{{smaller block|It would not be possible for Pemberton to attack me with all his troops at one place, and I determined to ''throw'' my army between his and fight him in detail.
{{right|''U. S. Grant'', Personal Memoirs, I. 496.}}}}

11. To bring forth; produce, as young; bear; cast: said especially of rabbits.

{{smaller block|When a pure race of white or black pigeons ''throws'' a slaty-blue bird . . . we are quite unable to assign any proximate cause.
{{right|''Darwin.''}}}}

{{smaller block|Mares that have done much hard work are not the best dams that can be selected, as they are apt to slip their foals, or to ''throw'' undersized ones.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XII. 188.}}}}

12. To make a cast with, as dice; play with, as dice; make (a cast of dice).

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Set less than thou ''throwest.''{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 4. 136.}}}}

{{smaller block|That great day of expense, in which a man is to ''throw'' his last cast for an eternity of joys or sorrows.{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), I. 533.}}}}

13. In ''card-playing'', to lay upon the table; play, as a card.—14. To turn; direct; cast: as, to ''throw'' one's eyes to the ground.

{{smaller block|Lo, what befel! he ''threw'' his eye aside.{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, iv. 3. 103.}}}}

15. To sell, as a race or game; allow another to win unnecessarily or in accordance with previous agreement.—{{smaller|'''Throw up''', in ''printing'', a direction to enlarge the size of a line of displayed type.—'''To throw across''', to construct across: as, to ''throw'' a bridge ''across'' a river.—'''To throw a levant†.''' See ''levant''<sup>3</sup>.—'''To throw a sop to Cerberus.''' See ''sop''.—'''To throw away.''' (''a'') To cast from one's hand; put suddenly out of one's hold or possession.}}

{{smaller block|The Duke took out the Knife, and ''threw'' it ''away.''{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. v. 7.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') To part with without compensation; give or spend recklessly; squander; lose by negligence or folly; waste.}}

{{smaller block|Dilatory fortune plays the jilt
With the brave, noble, honest, gallant man,
To ''throw'' herself ''away'' on fools and knaves.{{right|''Otway'', The Orphan, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|She ''threw away'' her money upon roaring bullies, that went about the streets.{{right|''Arbuthnot'', Hist. John Bull.}}}}

{{smaller block|It is bare justice to Clive to say that, proud and overbearing as he was, kindness was never ''thrown away'' upon him.{{right|''Macaulay'', Lord Clive.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
(c) To reject; refuse; lose by indifference or neglect: as, to ''throw away'' a good offer.—'''To throw back.''' (''a'') To reflect, as light, etc. (''b'') To reject; refuse. (''c'') To cast back, as a slur or an insinuation.—'''To throw by,''' to cast or lay aside as useless; discard.

{{smaller block|It can but shew<br>Like one of Juno's disguises; and<br>When things succeed be ''thrown by'', or let fall.
{{right|''B. Jonson.'' (''Johnson.'')}}}}

'''To throw cold water on.''' See ''cold.''—'''To throw down.''' (''a'') To cast to the ground or other lower position: as, the men ''threw down'' their tools. See ''to throw down the gauntlet'', under ''gauntlet''<sup>1</sup>.

{{smaller block|That with which K. Richard was charged, beside the Wrong done to Leopold in ''throwing down'' his Colours at Ptolemais, was the Death of Conrade Duke of Tyre.
{{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 64.}}}}

(''b'') To bring from an erect or exalted to a prostrate position or condition; hence, to overturn; subvert; demolish; destroy.

{{smaller block|Must one rash word, the infirmity of age,<br>''Throw down'' the merit of my better years?
{{right|''Addison'', Cato, ii. 5.}}}}

{{smaller block|In January 1740 they had three great shocks of an earthquake immediately after one another, which ''threw down'' some mosques and several houses.
{{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, I. 195.}}}}

'''To throw dust in one's eyes.''' See ''dust''<sup>1</sup>.—'''To throw in.''' (''a'') To cast or place within; insert; inject, as a fluid. (''b'') To put in or deposit along with another or others: as, he has ''thrown in'' his fortune with yours.

{{smaller block|We cannot ''throw in'' our lot with revolutionaries and with those who are guilty of treason to the Constitution and to the Empire.
{{right|''Edinburgh Rev.'', CLXV. 303.}}}}

(''c'') To interpolate: as, he ''threw in'' a word now and then. (''d'') To add without reckoning, or as if to complete or effect a bargain or sale: as, I will ''throw in'' this book if you buy the lot.—'''To throw into shape,''' to give form or arrangement to.

{{smaller block|It would be well to ''throw'' his notes and materials ''into''}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|some ''shape.'' {{right|''English Gilds'' (E. E. T. S.), Int., p. xii.}}}}

'''To throw into the bargain.''' Same as ''to throw in'' (''d''). —'''To throw light on,''' to make clear or intelligible.

{{smaller block|Lady Sarah Cowper has left a memorandum respecting her father, Lord Cowper, which ''throws light on'' this subject.
{{right|''J. Ashton,'' Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne, II. 141.}}}}

'''To throw off.''' (''a'') To cast off, away, or aside; divest one's self of hurriedly or carelessly; abandon the use of; free one's self of, as an impediment; get rid of, as a disease: as, to ''throw off'' one's clothes; to ''throw off'' all disguise; to ''throw off'' a cold or a fever.

{{smaller block|The free spirit of mankind at length
''Throws'' its last fetters ''off.'' {{right|''Bryant,'' The Ages.}}}}

{{smaller block|An eschar was formed, which was soon ''thrown off,'' leaving a healthy granulating surface.
{{right|''J. M. Carnochan,'' Operative Surgery, p. 46.}}}}

(''b'') To discard; dismiss: as, to ''throw off'' an acquaintance or a dependent. (''c'') To do or say in a rapid offhand manner: as, to ''throw off'' a poem. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|Often Addison's most brilliant efforts are built upon a chance hint ''thrown off'' at random by Steele's hurrying pen.
{{right|''A. Dobson,'' Int. to Steele, p. xxx.}}}}

'''To throw on,''' to put on or don hastily or carelessly: as, he ''threw on'' his cloak.—'''To throw one's self down,''' to lie down.—'''To throw one's self into,''' to engage heartily, earnestly, or vigorously in: as, he ''threw himself into'' the contest, and did good service.—'''To throw one's self on''' or '''upon,''' to cast one's faith or confidence upon; trust or resign one's self to, as for favor or protection; repose upon: as, to ''throw one's self on'' the mercy of the court.

{{smaller block|In time of temptation be not busy to dispute, but . . . ''throw yourself upon'' God. ''Jer. Taylor,'' Holy Living, iv. 1.}}

'''To throw open.''' (''a'') To open suddenly or widely.

{{smaller block|"Who knocks?" cried Goodman Garvin.
The door was ''open thrown.''
{{right|''Whittier,'' Mary Garvin.}}}}

(''b'') To give free or unrestricted access to; remove all barriers, obstacles, or restrictions from: as, the appoint-

readings.c3s3:
ment was ''thrown open'' to public competition.—'''To throw open the door to.''' See ''door.''—'''To throw out.''' (''a'') To cast out; expel; reject or discard.

{{smaller block|Admit that Monarchy of itself may be convenient to som Nations; yet to us who have ''thrown'' it ''out'', receiv'd back again, it cannot but prove pernicious.
{{right|''Milton'', Free Commonwealth.}}}}

(''b'') To cause to project, or to become prominent; build out: as, to ''throw out'' a pier or landing-stage, or a wing of a building. (''c'') To emit: as, that lamp ''throws out'' a bright light. (''d'') To give utterance to; insinuate: as, to ''throw out'' a hint.

{{smaller block|I have ''thrown out'' words
That would have fetch'd warm blood upon the cheeks
Of guilty men, and he is never mov'd.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Maid's Tragedy, iv. 2.}}}}

(''e'') To put off the right track; confuse; embarrass: as, interruption ''throws'' one ''out''. (''f'') To leave behind; distance: as, a horse ''thrown'' completely ''out'' of the race. (''g'') To reject; exclude: as, the bill was ''thrown out'' on the second reading. (''h'') In ''printing'', to reject or throw aside, as printed sheets that are imperfect. (''i'') In ''base-ball'', to put out, as a base-runner, by a ball fielded to one of the players on or near a base. (''j'') In ''cricket'', to put out (a batsman) when he is out of his ground by a fielder hitting the wicket.—'''To throw over,''' to desert; abandon; neglect. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|They say the Rads are going to ''throw'' us ''over''.
{{right|''Disraeli'', Coningsby.}}}}

{{smaller block|Saddled with a vast number of engagements, any of which (and this made him none the less popular) he was ready to ''throw over'' at a moment's notice.
{{right|''Whyte Melville'', White Rose, II. xi.}}}}
